 1.2 kilometers separating the two. The station shares several critical infrastructure systems with Station 36, including Ventilation System A and the fire suppression system, ensuring coordinated safety and operational efficiency between the two stations. Additionally, Station 35 is connected to the Central Power Grid via Transformer 35A and is integrated into the metro’s Signaling System, which is managed by Central Traffic Control.\n\nThe station is primarily ventilated by Ventilation System A, making its operational status crucial for the safety and comfort of passengers. On March 15, 2026, Station 35 was notably affected by a ventilation failure involving this system, which necessitated the evacuation of 3,200 passengers and led to a temporary closure of the station. This incident underscored the importance of the shared ventilation and fire suppression systems with Station 36.\n\nStation 35 is maintained by Shapir Engineering, with Yossi Cohen designated as the responsible person overseeing its operations and maintenance. The station’s design and operational protocols reflect its role as a major transit hub within the Kiryat Arie business district, providing essential connectivity for both business and residential commuters in Petah Tikva.", "STATION 36:Station 36, also known as Shderot HaTzionut, is a deep underground metro station situated at the intersection of Jabotinsky Street and Shderot HaTzionut in Petah Tikva, Israel. Serving the Nachalat Yehuda neighborhood and surrounding residential areas, Station 36 is part of the Israel M1 Metro Line. The station is located 25 meters below ground and features a platform that is 150 meters long and 12 meters wide, accommodating a daily passenger capacity of up to 45,000.\n\nStation 36 is designed with three main entrances: North, South, and Central Plaza, providing convenient access for commuters. It is strategically positioned as the adjacent station to Station 35, with a 1.2-kilometer tunnel connecting the two. Both stations share critical infrastructure, including Ventilation System A and the Fire Suppression System, ensuring safety and operational efficiency. The station is primarily ventilated by Ventilation System A, making its operational status vital to the station’s functionality.\n\nConstructed and maintained by Shapir Engineering, Station 36 is overseen by Yossi Cohen, who is responsible for its ongoing maintenance and safety. The station is integrated with various critical systems and is connected to adjacent stations, supporting the smooth flow of metro traffic in the area.\n\nOn March 15, 2026, Station 36 was notably affected by a ventilation failure involving Ventilation System A. This incident necessitated the evacuation of 4,100 passengers and led to a temporary closure of the station to ensure passenger safety and restore proper ventilation. The event highlighted the importance of the shared infrastructure between Station 36 and Station 35, as well as the essential role of the station’s safety and emergency systems.\n\nIn summary, Station 36 (Shderot HaTzionut) is a key underground metro station on the Israel M1 Metro Line, serving as a major transit hub for Petah Tikva’s Nachalat Yehuda neighborhood. With its modern design, high passenger capacity, and robust safety systems, it plays a crucial role in the city’s public transportation network. Its construction and maintenance by Shapir Engineering, along with the oversight of Yossi Cohen, ensure that Station 36 remains a vital and secure component of the metro system, despite challenges such as the 2026 ventilation failure.", "VENTILATION SYSTEM A:VENTILATION SYSTEM A is a critical shared infrastructure system on the Israel M1 Metro Line, providing essential ventilation services to both Station 35 and Station 36. This system is responsible for ensuring fresh air circulation, smoke extraction during emergencies, and temperature control within these stations, directly impacting the safety and comfort of approximately 80,000 daily passengers. \n\nThe system comprises main and backup fans, air shafts, and an automated control system that includes a manual override feature. Its operation is dependent on several key subsystems, including the Central Power Grid, Emergency Generators, the SCADA Control System, and the Fire Detection System. The integration of these components ensures that Ventilation System A can respond effectively to both routine and emergency situations.\n\nA failure of Ventilation System A has significant consequences, as it results in the loss of ventilation for both Station 35 and Station 36. Such a failure can lead to reduced operational capacity or necessitate the immediate evacuation of the affected stations to ensure passenger safety. This was exemplified on March 15, 2026, when the system experienced a complete failure due to a degraded power supply unit, which led to the evacuation of both stations.\n\nMaintenance of Ventilation System A is managed by Climatec Systems, which is responsible for ensuring the reliability and functionality of the system. The critical nature of Ventilation System A underscores its importance in the overall infrastructure of the Israel M1 Metro Line, as its proper operation is vital for maintaining safe and comfortable conditions for passengers and staff at both Station 35 and Station 36.", "CLIMATEC SYSTEMS:Climatec Systems is the maintenance contractor responsible for Ventilation System A, including replacing power supply units and performing repairs.", "SHAPIR ENGINEERING:Shapir Engineering is the construction contractor responsible for the building of Station 35 and Station 36 on the M1 Metro Line. Their scope of work includes the construction of both stations, utilizing the cut-and-cover method, as well as the use of Tunnel Boring Machine (TBM) tunnel methods specifically for Station 36. The construction period for these projects spans from 2019 to 2024. In addition to the physical construction, Shapir Engineering is also responsible for the tunneling work associated with the M1 Metro Line and oversees emergency procedures and station management for Station 35 and Station 36.", "YOSSI COHEN:Yossi Cohen is a project manager at Shapir Engineering, responsible for overseeing the construction of both Station 35 and Station 36. As the designated project manager for these stations, Yossi Cohen represents Shapir Engineering in all matters related to their development. His role encompasses managing the construction processes, coordinating project activities, and ensuring that both Station 35 and Station 36 are completed according to the required standards and timelines. Through his leadership, Yossi Cohen plays a key part in the successful execution of Shapir Engineering’s projects at these locations.", "NTA CONTROL CENTER:NTA Control Center is the operational control system that received SCADA alerts, coordinated the emergency response, and is responsible for overall incident management.", "VENTILATION FAILURE - MARCH 15, 2026:A major incident on March 15, 2026, where Ventilation System A failed, causing the evacuation of Station 35 and Station 36, affecting 7,300 passengers and disrupting service for over 2 hours.", "EMERGENCY GENERATORS:Emergency Generators are backup power systems designed to automatically activate during ventilation system failures, ensuring the continued operation of critical infrastructure. Specifically, these generators provide backup power to Ventilation System A, offering a 30-minute capacity to support safe evacuation procedures in the event of a failure. At Station 35 and Station 36, Emergency Generators supply temporary power to affected systems, maintaining essential functions and safety until normal power is restored or evacuation is complete. Their automatic activation and targeted support for ventilation and other critical systems make Emergency Generators a vital component of emergency preparedness and response at these stations.", "POWER SUPPLY UNIT:The main power supply unit is a critical component of Ventilation System A, whose failure due to capacitor degradation caused the incident on March 15, 2026.", "AIR QUALITY SENSORS:Air quality sensors are monitoring devices at Station 35 and Station 36 that detected reduced airflow immediately after the ventilation system failure.", "NTA:NTA is the organization responsible for the overall operation and management of the transit system, including the NTA Control Center and implementation of system-wide safety and redundancy measures.", "SCADA:", "KIRYAT ARIE:Kiryat Arie is the name of Station 35 on the Israel M1 Metro Line, served by Ventilation System A.", "SHDEROT HATZIONUT:Shderot HaTzionut refers both to Station 36 on the Israel M1 Metro Line and to the street after which the station is named. Station 36, known as Shderot HaTzionut, is served by Ventilation System A and is situated at an intersection that includes Shderot HaTzionut street. The station’s name reflects its location, providing a clear reference point for passengers and connecting the metro system to the surrounding urban environment.", "ISRAEL M1 METRO LINE:The Israel M1 Metro Line is a metro line in Israel, with stations including Station 35 (Kiryat Arie) and Station 36 (Shderot HaTzionut), both served by Ventilation System A."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_7036b818d8887cac52fe82f5849183c3803ca1e285bd9b81abf3cf97e833e6cc_v2"}